hion show and not being sat  on the front row with your peers i mean  that was quite brave of you  when you look at my work from the age of  18 i've always  been very true to myself i'd be always  be very authentic about my feelings  in that particular case you know when  you walk into a show  and all fashion directors are in the  front row and you're in the second world  straight away that's you  you know profiling me um even  a few weeks ago the same thing sort of  happened the incident that you're  referring to  in the office when you're racially  profiled by a security guard in your  your own office  how did you feel when that happened it  wasn't an isolated incident of course  you know it takes you back to all those  moments in your life when that has  happened  and i think people people think that  when you're successful  that things like that don't happen for  me the beauty of that incident is it  just makes me aware  never to think i'm too successful  that it can happen any day and i have to  be really alert  and deal with it really and what would  you say to  to those that say you know your your  success is  an armor against racism and you know  things like that don't happen to to  people like you  but obviously as we saw the other day  does happen from my tweets  it's important that something like this  did happen because it reminds me  never to think i'm yeah like i said too  successful  or too well known or too established  because  to somebody you're another black person  and i really didn't want all the young  kids in  the building to have to face that first  black  editor of british vogue first male  editor of british vogue um lots of  firsts  in your your armory um are you  comfortable  with being described as a pioneer  oh god okay um  i just like to think that you know i  i've contributed to the conversation  i've contributed to you know the world  of fashion and imagery  and by doing that i've been able to sort  of shine their lives on  shining light on the marginalized  marginalized groups you know people  who've been othered  that's really what i i'm most proud of  making  everybody a part of the conversation and  are you happy  to um be described as political that the  magazine  does have a statement to make now about  our current conditions  i wouldn't say political but i think we  you i do use the platform  to address injustices um you know to  talk about you know  people's rights so you know like we said  before you know  social rights you know trans rights you  know the people we feature you know  zadie smith salmon we're not shy we  don't shy away from controversy but  on the other hand it's all through a  vogue lens  to the highest quality and you know  running alongside all that we still have  the incredible fashion stories the  incredible  images you know designer profiles for me  and my team  who are sort of a diverse team the mix  is the most important for me  tell us about the september issue the  the concept the idea behind it  and and what has materialized so the  september issue is a very special one  we went from sort of we went from you  know covey to talking about  environmental issues  to the biggest moment in civil rights  history i mean  the whole world of the marches  and i felt like these incredible  people who were sort of  given their time and their lives to make  sure  that there was social justice in the  world to make sure that there was  education and community care  these were the people who were the new  heroes  and what we perhaps previously deemed as  ordinary  became extraordinary ordinary is now so  extraordinary  and that's what what i love about what  we're able to do about  you know we've been able to pivot still  keep it vogue but talk about issues that  really  matter as well and the september issue  um the front cover tell us who's on the  front cover and and also the the  collaborations be  behind the scenes as well yes i mean the  issue was shot by  the cover was 